fix(windows): read the presenter from the first-frame line, not the open line

The first Windows CI run went green, and the log showed why it must not
have: the assertion was reading the wrong line.

    info: Windows window: 480x360 (glinlandui), presenter: d3d11 (hardware)
    info: Windows first frame: 480x360, 172800 painted pixels, gdi upload checksum ...
    demo painted 172800 pixels, ... (presenter: d3d11)

Two lines, two different presenters. `run()` logs the presenter as soon
as the device is CREATED; the shim only discovers it cannot actually
present when it presents, and falls back to GDI. On a hosted runner the
D3D11 device is real-looking but cannot execute a shader, so the window
opened, reported d3d11, then composited with GDI.

The step grepped `presenter:` and took the first match — the open line —
so it asserted `d3d11` for a frame that GDI drew. A gate that reports the
happy path when the fallback is what ran is worse than no gate, which is
the same mistake ci/check_windows_colors.c exists to prevent. It now
reads the presenter from the first-frame line, which is reported after
the present and is therefore authoritative. A machine whose presenter is
neither d3d11 nor gdi still fails the step.

Also: the colour probe now selects its device the way the WINDOW does —
hardware first, WARP second — instead of WARP first. The symmetry is the
probe's entire premise: a check that exercised a different adapter than
the window measures something else. The WARP-first ordering was a guess
made from one broken local machine, and it was the less representative
of the two.

No behaviour change on a healthy machine. Verified locally: fmt clean,
zig build test 519/534, check-colors still reports its skip honestly on
a host whose D3D11 cannot rasterize.
